WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.960
As it shows, I got a ton done, like an unbelievable amount done.

00:04.960 --> 00:09.360
This is the closed PR's in Lakebed where, reminder, I'm still the only contributor.

00:09.360 --> 00:12.000
I have not forced this project onto my team yet.

00:12.000 --> 00:13.400
They don't deserve the slop.

00:13.400 --> 00:14.400
It's cleaning up fast.

00:14.400 --> 00:15.560
Thank you, Fable.

00:15.560 --> 00:20.320
But all of these PR's were done in literally two to three days.

00:20.320 --> 00:25.120
The sheer volume of work here, especially if you're looking at the ones that actually merged

00:25.120 --> 00:31.800
like this pile here. It's like I think 11 or 12 PRs that merged all not just in one day,

00:31.800 --> 00:37.600
but from one thread. And I keep going and I'm on page two. We're still in things from

00:37.600 --> 00:43.280
two days ago. Then there's a four day ago, one five days ago, then a huge jump to two weeks

00:43.280 --> 00:48.480
because I was just not really feeling it. When I lost access to this model, I tried to

00:48.480 --> 00:52.120
run around in circles and get the best stuff I could out of Opus, but it wasn't at the

00:52.120 --> 00:53.560
the bar I needed to ship.

00:53.560 --> 00:56.120
It what ended up happening is in this particular project,

00:56.120 --> 00:59.600
Lakebed, I had a bunch of these PR stack up of work

00:59.600 --> 01:02.320
that was like 50 to 80% complete,

01:02.320 --> 01:04.360
but I didn't like the SDK changes it made.

01:04.360 --> 01:06.960
I didn't really have confidence in the implementation,

01:06.960 --> 01:09.120
including it up would just take too long.

01:09.120 --> 01:11.000
And the result was that I kept having ideas,

01:11.000 --> 01:14.080
I kept making PRs, and they kept sitting there doing nothing.

01:14.080 --> 01:18.560
I probably had 20 or 30 PRs just sitting in this repo,

01:18.560 --> 01:21.160
not moving, and the project has been sitting there

01:21.160 --> 01:22.800
as a result, and I don't like that.

01:22.800 --> 01:25.040
I really want to ship Lakebed.

01:25.040 --> 01:26.840
I've talked about what Lakebed is in some other videos.

01:26.840 --> 01:28.600
I don't want to bog you down the details

01:28.600 --> 01:29.800
until I actually have it launched.

01:29.800 --> 01:31.280
I haven't earned the right to market this yet.

01:31.280 --> 01:32.160
I want it done.

01:32.160 --> 01:33.800
So we'll do that later.

01:33.800 --> 01:35.400
But I want to talk about the workflows

01:35.400 --> 01:37.760
that got me this far in the project,

01:37.760 --> 01:39.280
as well as some of the cool things I'm doing

01:39.280 --> 01:41.440
on other projects that I've been working on.

01:41.440 --> 01:43.160
I'm going to start in a weird place here,

01:43.160 --> 01:44.680
but I promise it will help.

01:44.680 --> 01:46.640
I'm going to start with min-maxing tips

01:46.640 --> 01:48.400
and like cost reduction things

01:48.400 --> 01:50.960
in order to make sure that when you adopt this workflow,

01:50.960 --> 01:53.200
you don't immediately bankrupt yourself,

01:53.200 --> 01:55.600
because if you were to go to the second half of this video

01:55.600 --> 01:58.440
and skip straight to that part, it will be expensive.

01:58.440 --> 02:00.400
That's what I did in the first three days.

